<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of East Frisia</h1><p class="st-meta">1464 CE – 1744 CE · capital Aurich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The North Sea county of the Cirksena family, whose powerful estates and Calvinist port of Emden kept their rulers in check.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After a century of feuds among Frisian chieftains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-i-count-of-east-frisia">Ulrich Cirksena</a> united most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a>, and in 1464 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Frederick III</a> made him an imperial count. His descendants extended their rule over neighbouring chieftaincies, though they failed to win the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-jever">Lordship of Jever</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">Cirksena</a>, raised to princes in the 17th century, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurich">Aurich</a>, but the estates, above all the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a>, held great power. Emden became a Calvinist stronghold, the '<a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> of the North', and in 1595 rebelled against the Lutheran counts, after which the estates leaned on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, grain and shipping.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edzard-prince-of-east-frisia">Charles Edzard</a>, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">Cirksena</a>, died in 1744, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick II of Prussia</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> under an inheritance claim. It passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hanover">Kingdom of Hanover</a> in 1815 and back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866, and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-east-frisia.jpg" alt="County of East Frisia" width="320" height="371"><figcaption>Image: TotaFrisia · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EastFrisia1625.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1464 CE – 1744 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aurich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ulrich I, Edzard the Great, Charles Edzard</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of East Frisia, Ostfriesland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1604&amp;focus=county-of-east-frisia" aria-label="County of East Frisia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4840 342 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of East Frisia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5160 598L5229 694M5160 598L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5160 598h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5160 598h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1604&amp;focus=county-of-east-frisia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
